`useState` and other hooks rely on the React call stack to determine the context in which they are called. When a functional component renders, React keeps track of the order in which hooks are called. This is why the rules of hooks require that hooks are called in the same order on every render and that they are not called conditionally.

Here's a simplified example to illustrate how React might keep track of state:

```jsx
const state = [];
let cursor = 0;

function useState(initialValue) {
  const currentCursor = cursor;
  if (state[currentCursor] === undefined) {
    state[currentCursor] = initialValue;
  }
  const setState = (newValue) => {
    state[currentCursor] = newValue;
    render(); // re-render the component
  };
  cursor++;
  return [state[currentCursor], setState];
}

function render() {
  cursor = 0; // reset the cursor before each render
  App(); // call the component function
}

function App() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);
  console.log(count);
  // ... rest of the component
}
```

In this example, `state` is an array that holds the state values, and `cursor` is a variable that keeps track of the index of the current state value in the `state` array. `useState` uses `cursor` to access the correct state value from the `state` array. After each call to `useState`, `cursor` is incremented so that the next call to `useState` will access the next state value in the array.

This is a simplified example and not how React actually implements hooks, but it should give you an idea of how React can use the call stack to keep track of state values between renders.
